From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:50:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] leave a camera's output as the camera wrote it

A buffer the camera writes was taken for a buffer the host writes, so every CPU mapping of it for
reading transferred the host copy over the guest pages. The camera device writes the guest pages of
a frame, the way the video decoder does and for the same reason a decoder's output needed
"leave a decoder's output as the decoder wrote it": the transfer replaces the picture with whatever
the host copy held.

The camera flag no longer makes a mapping transfer from the host. A buffer that is also rendered
into still does.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 virtgpu_virgl.c | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/virtgpu_virgl.c b/virtgpu_virgl.c
index cdb3c67b32be3b3b86cf66f94852b43b2f472077..bc759949efd1973cba48c49eca7db8e534dacd88 100644
--- a/virtgpu_virgl.c
+++ b/virtgpu_virgl.c
@@ -1340,8 +1340,10 @@ static int virgl_bo_invalidate(struct bo *bo, struct mapping *mapping)
 	// use the format to determine whether this buffer could be encoder/decoder output.
 	// A decoder writes the guest pages of its output buffer and never the host copy, so
 	// a transfer from the host would replace the decoded picture with stale contents,
-	// and the decoder flag is not a host write.
-	host_write_flags = BO_USE_RENDERING | BO_USE_CAMERA_WRITE | BO_USE_GPU_DATA_BUFFER;
+	// and the decoder flag is not a host write. The camera device does the same: it writes
+	// the guest pages of a frame, so a mapping for reading must not transfer the host copy
+	// over them, and the camera flag is not a host write either.
+	host_write_flags = BO_USE_RENDERING | BO_USE_GPU_DATA_BUFFER;
 	if (bo->meta.format == DRM_FORMAT_R8)
 		host_write_flags |= BO_USE_HW_VIDEO_ENCODER;
 
